      "content": "Escrow, in the context of buying a house, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that the transaction unfolds smoothly, much like a team of skilled, yet mischievous, goblins working together to complete a complex puzzle. You see, in the world of real estate, there are many moving parts, and escrow helps to keep everything in check, just like a goblin's clever trap-making skills keep their underground lairs secure.\n\nIn Toronto, where the goblins of the financial district are always on the lookout for a good deal, escrow is a crucial component of the home-buying process. It's essentially a holding tank for funds that will be released when the sale is complete and all conditions are met. Think of it like a goblin's treasure chest, where the loot (in this case, the funds) is safely stored until the right moment to be released.\n\nFor example, let's say you're buying a house in Toronto, and the seller is requesting a certain amount of money for repairs or credits. In this case, the escrow account would hold the funds until the repairs are completed or the credits are applied. It's a bit like a g",
